WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.857
Use this checkpoint to test the contracts.

00:00:02.857 --> 00:00:08.237
A comparison such as age greater than parentheses requires one scalar value.

00:00:08.237 --> 00:00:10.622
IN expects a one-column set.

00:00:10.622 --> 00:00:14.332
EXISTS only needs to know whether any row exists.

00:00:14.332 --> 00:00:19.047
And a subquery is correlated when it references the current outer row.

00:00:19.047 --> 00:00:25.067
If you can identify these contracts, the syntax becomes much easier to reconstruct.

00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:29.047
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:29.047 --> 00:00:34.142
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:34.142 --> 00:00:37.747
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:37.747 --> 00:00:42.142
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:48.867
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:52.962
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:52.962 --> 00:00:57.440
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
